<h2>The scheme, as the prosecution describes it</h2>
<p>M/s Welfra Investment and Assets Management Pvt. Ltd. is alleged to have offered portfolio management services without SEBI registration, promising market-linked returns. SEBI&rsquo;s findings put about Rs. 12.96 crore in credits to the company&rsquo;s account between 2021 and 2024. The arrested and charge-sheeted director allegedly named the petitioner — one of four equal shareholders — as the person who handled clients&rsquo; demat accounts and received money from investors into company accounts. The State attributed to him executed portfolio management agreements, a Rs. 5 lakh security cheque, and an email concerning demat account details. Eighteen complainants claim aggregate losses of about Rs. 2 crore.</p>

<h2>Defence and prosecution, side by side</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Petitioner</h3>
    <p>Not named in the FIR; resigned as director on 6 March 2023; was not an authorised signatory; WhatsApp messages show he objected to the company&rsquo;s conduct; his own mother invested about Rs. 9.38 lakh and he sent a refund notice; the evidence is documentary, and the main accused is already charge-sheeted.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>State</h3>
    <p>Risk of threats to complainants and of absconding — he is a resident of Cooch Behar with no permanent Delhi address; more victims may come forward; a similar FIR in Faridabad shows a pattern; the money trail and his precise role still need custodial interrogation.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>The Court&rsquo;s reasoning</h2>
<p>The Court viewed the matter through the economic-offences lens: <em>&ldquo;Economic offences of such a nature are to be viewed seriously and treated as a class apart.&rdquo;</em> Three strands carried the decision. First, scale: several complainants, a substantial corpus, and the realistic prospect of more victims. Second, role: resignation from the board did not account for the period before March 2023, when the agreements and demat operations attributed to him occurred. Third, method: <em>&ldquo;the existence of documentary evidence does not, by itself, obviate the need for custodial interrogation.&rdquo;</em> The precedents applied — Srikant Upadhyay, Mhetre, Krishna Mohan Reddy, and the Chidambaram line on economic offences — are the standard suite, and the conclusion followed: <em>&ldquo;I do not consider this to be a fit case for the grant of anticipatory bail.&rdquo;</em></p>

<h2>What this means for investment-scheme matters</h2>
<p>Two practice points stand out. For persons connected to a company under EOW investigation, formal exit — resignation, transfer of shareholding — limits prospective exposure but does nothing for the period of alleged participation; the record of that period, not the exit, decides the bail question. And for investors on the other side, the order shows the material the EOW builds on: SEBI&rsquo;s findings on registration, account credits, agreements bearing signatures, and statements of co-accused. A complaint supported by those elements moves; a bare grievance about returns does not.</p>
<div class="note">Observations in an anticipatory bail order are confined to that application and are not findings on guilt. Case details are as recorded in the order dated 5 November 2026.</div>
